<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbasid Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/750">750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbasid Revolution was the uprising that overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> and brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid dynasty</a> to power in 750. Launched from Khurasan under the organiser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, it drew on discontent among non-Arab Muslims and supporters of the Prophet's family. It ended Umayyad rule in the east, shifted the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, and is central to the foundation of the Abbasid Caliphate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution.jpg" alt="Abbasid Revolution" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sheperd, William R.; Historical Atlas; New York: Henry Holt and Company, 1911. 53. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caliphate_750.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the 740s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> was in critical condition. A succession crisis in 744 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-fitna">Third Fitna</a>, which raged across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> for three years, and a Kharijite rebellion and a revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a>'s move of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> followed. Marwan II pacified the provinces only in 747, and the revolution began within months.</p><p>Support came from many backgrounds. Non-Arab Muslims resented their marginal position: they could not live in the garrison cities, hold officer posts or work for the government, and still paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> resented centralised authority over their nomadic lifestyle, and Shi'as remembered the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> used extensively to win support. Non-Muslim subjects, barred from government posts by a decree of 741, also backed the cause.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From about 719 missions of the Hashimiyya movement sought adherents in Khurasan, asking support for a member of the House of Muhammad who would be pleasing to everyone, without naming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. On 9 June 747 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> began an open revolt under the Black Standard, with close to 10,000 soldiers. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> on 14 February 748 and expelled the governor Nasr ibn Sayyar. His officer Qahtaba ibn Shabib al-Ta'i defeated an Umayyad force of 10,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a> in August 748 and one of 50,000 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in March 749.</p><p>In August 749 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> raided the camp of the Umayyad commander Yazid ibn Umar al-Fazari at night. Qahtaba was killed, but al-Fazari fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>, where his troops were besieged from that August to July 750. Most of the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> were hunted down and killed. When Abd Allah ibn Ali offered amnesty, eighty of them gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and were massacred, and he presented the killings as retaliation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 755 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> executed for heresy and treason, which provoked rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>. Non-Muslims regained government posts in a more cosmopolitan empire centred on the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Bernard Lewis points out that, though the revolution is often called a Persian victory and an Arab defeat, the caliph remained an Arab, Arabic remained the language of administration, and the Arab nobility kept its land. No contemporary account survives, and most sources were written more than a century later under Abbasid rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbasid revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Abbasid Caliphate was founded in 750 CE following the <b>Abbasid Revolution</b>, which overthrew the Umayyad dynasty at the Battle of the Great Zab in January of that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>No other choice of the dynasty had greater political consequences: it generated deep resentment among Persian, Berber and other converts, which the <b>Abbasid Revolution</b> would later mobilise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He was a fugitive and one of the last remaining members of the Umayyad royal family, which had ruled the first hereditary caliphate from Damascus until it was overthrown in the <b>Abbasid Revolution</b> of 750.</blockquote><a href="/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
